From 766db3c02bf0127693734e0b59c422cbda0dd112 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@localhost>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 23:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 29/30] Single texel alignment emulation with argument buffers

vkd3d-proton requires storage/uniformTexelBufferOffsetSingleTexelAlignment; Metal texture buffers need 16-byte
aligned offsets on Apple GPUs. The fork's emulation (the Metal texture starts at the aligned offset below the
view, the shader adds the remaining texels) was off: disabled with Metal argument buffers ("incompatible"),
and add_texture_buffer_offsets was never set for SPIRV-Cross, so shaders ignored the offsets anyway.

- add_texture_buffer_offsets follows emulatedTexelBufferAlignment; the emulation stays on with argument buffers.
- Argument buffer descriptor sets keep the texel offset of each texel buffer descriptor in the set's aux buffer
  (where buffer sizes are), at the index of its texture, written with the descriptor and copied with it (also
  for variable-count bindings). SPIRV-Cross reads them from spvBufferSizeConstants.
- Sets bound without argument buffers (push descriptors): texel buffer descriptors keep the offset (OneIDMeta),
  the implicit texture offset buffer is indexed by texture index (SPIRV-Cross reads it at the texture's index;
  it was filled densely per texel buffer) and push descriptors fill it too.
- Buffer sizes in the aux buffer were written at the index of the update's first element plus i instead of the
  element (an update starting at dstArrayElement > 0 overwrote the sizes of the first elements: arrayLength and
  robust bounds of storage buffer arrays).
